Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from the novella by Charles Dickens
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Marley was dead: to begin with. There is no doubt whatever about that. Old Marley was as dead as a door-nail.

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY'S COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

Fog presses at the windows. A small fire gutters in the grate. EBENEZER SCROOGE — gaunt, sharp-nosed, sixty, wrapped in a shabby greatcoat — sits at a high desk, counting coins by candlelight. Through a glass partition, his clerk BOB CRATCHIT, thin, threadbare, thirty-something, copies letters by a fire no bigger than one coal.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Scrooge! a squeezing, wrenching, grasping, scraping, clutching, covetous old sinner! Secret, and self-contained, and solitary as an oyster.

The door bursts open. FRED, Scrooge's nephew, ruddy-cheeked and beaming, strides in, breath smoking.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. What right have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
Come, then. What right have you to be dismal? What reason have you to be morose? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
Bah. Humbug.

Fred laughs it off, undeterred.

FRED
I have always thought of Christmas time as a good time; a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time — the only time I know of when men and women seem by one consent to open their shut-up hearts freely. And therefore, uncle, I say, God bless it!

Bob Cratchit, unseen in the Tank, applauds involuntarily, then hastily pokes the fire to cover himself.

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation.

(to Fred)

Good afternoon.

FRED
Dine with us to-morrow, uncle.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
I want nothing from you; I ask nothing of you; why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred sighs, unoffended, and turns to go.

FRED
A Merry Christmas, uncle — and A Happy New Year!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred exits past Bob, exchanging warm holiday wishes with him at the door. Two portly gentlemen, hats in hand, papers under their arms, step past Fred into the office — MR. ASHBY and his silent companion.

MR. ASHBY
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe. At this festive season, sir, it is desirable we make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute.

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons?

MR. ASHBY
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses — still in operation?

MR. ASHBY
They are. I wish I could say they were not.

SCROOGE
Oh, I'm very glad to hear it. What shall I put you down for?

MR. ASHBY
You wish to be anonymous?

SCROOGE
I wish to be left alone. If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population. Good afternoon, gentlemen!

The gentlemen withdraw, defeated. The fog thickens outside the window. A boy's voice, thin and cold, pipes at the door — a Christmas carol begun. Scrooge seizes his ruler and brandishes it toward the sound; the singing stops abruptly.

Evening. The office empties. Scrooge dons his coat.

SCROOGE (CONT'D)
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose.

BOB CRATCHIT
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair. A poor excuse for picking a man's pocket every twenty-fifth of December. But I suppose you must have the whole day. Be here all the earlier next morning.

Bob nods, gathering his long white comforter around him, no greatcoat against the cold. Scrooge walks out with a growl.

EXT. SCROOGE'S GATEWAY - NIGHT

Fog and frost cling to a dark, narrow yard. Scrooge fumbles his key into the door. He glances up at the great brass knocker.

It is not a knocker. It is Marley's face — livid, ghostly spectacles pushed up on its ghostly forehead, hair stirred as if by hot breath, eyes wide and motionless.

Scrooge freezes. He blinks — and it is a knocker again.

SCROOGE
Pooh, pooh.

He turns the key, walks in, and slams the door with a bang that rings through the whole dark house like thunder.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

Scrooge, candle in hand, checks every room — sitting-room, bedroom, lumber-room. Nobody under the table. Nobody under the bed. Satisfied, he double-locks his door — not his custom — dons his nightcap and dressing-gown, and sits before a low fire with a small saucepan of gruel.

An old disused bell hanging on the wall begins, inexplicably, to swing. It rings out, and every bell in the house rings with it. Then silence — followed by a clanking, as of a chain dragged over casks far below. The sound climbs the cellar stairs, floor by floor, straight toward the door.

SCROOGE
It's humbug still. I won't believe it.

The door opens without a hand upon it. MARLEY'S GHOST enters — the same face, pigtail, waistcoat, boots, transparent, a long chain of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, and ledgers wound about his middle.

SCROOGE (CONT'D)
How now! What do you want with me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Much.

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you, then?

MARLEY'S GHOST
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

Scrooge studies the transparent shape, unconvinced.

SCROOGE
You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese. There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are.

The Ghost gives a frightful cry and shakes its chain. It unwinds the bandage from its head — its lower jaw drops onto its breast. Scrooge falls to his knees.

SCROOGE (CONT'D)
Mercy! Why do you trouble me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellow men. Mine never did. And weary journeys lie before me!

SCROOGE
But you were always a good man of business, Jacob.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Business! Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business; charity, mercy, forbearance, benevolence, were, all, my business. The dealings of my trade were but a drop of water in the comprehensive ocean of my business!

The Ghost raises its chain and lets it fall.

MARLEY'S GHOST (CONT'D)
Hear me. My time is nearly gone. I am here to-night to warn you that you have yet a chance and hope of escaping my fate. You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
Is that the chance and hope, Jacob?

MARLEY'S GHOST
It is. Expect the first to-morrow when the bell tolls One. The second the next night at the same hour. The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate. Look to see me no more — and look that, for your own sake, you remember what has passed between us.

The Ghost binds its jaw again and backs toward the window, which rises open of itself. Scrooge follows and looks out — the night air is filled with wandering, chained phantoms, moaning, powerless now to help the living. The vision fades. Scrooge closes the window, checks the still double-locked door, and — exhausted — falls asleep in his clothes.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A church bell tolls One. Light floods the room. The bed-curtains are drawn aside by a hand. Scrooge sits up, face to face with the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST — strange, child-sized yet old-faced, a jet of clear light springing from its crown, an extinguisher-cap tucked beneath its arm.

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am. I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past. Rise, and walk with me.

The Spirit takes his arm. Scrooge clutches its robe in fear.

SCROOGE
I am a mortal, and liable to fall.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Bear but a touch of my hand there — and you shall be upheld in more than this.

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD AND SCHOOL - DAY

They pass through the wall and stand upon a snowy country road. The city has vanished entirely.

SCROOGE
Good Heaven! I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
These are but shadows of the things that have been. They have no consciousness of us.

They walk to a dreary red-brick mansion, and through to a bare schoolroom. A solitary BOY — Scrooge as a child — sits alone, reading, near a feeble fire. Scrooge weeps.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST (CONT'D)
The school is not quite deserted. A solitary child, neglected by his friends, is left there still.

The door bursts open. FAN, a small girl, darts in and throws her arms around the boy's neck.

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, for good and all! Father is so much kinder than he used to be, that home's like Heaven!

YOUNG SCROOGE
You are quite a woman, little Fan!

They depart together in a chaise, gay and laughing.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Always a delicate creature, whom a breath might have withered. But she had a large heart.

SCROOGE
So she had. You're right, Spirit. I will not gainsay it.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
She died a woman, and had, as I think, children.

SCROOGE
One child. My nephew.

Scrooge looks uneasy.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - NIGHT

A cluttered counting-house transforms in a blink into a warm, lamplit ballroom. Old FEZZIWIG, fat and jovial in a Welsh wig, claps his hands.

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick! No more work to-night! Let's have the shutters up before a man can say Jack Robinson!

A YOUNG SCROOGE — an apprentice now — and DICK WILKINS charge out and back with the shutters in a whirlwind. Fiddlers strike up. MRS. FEZZIWIG and dancers fill the floor. Scrooge, watching, is transfixed with pleasure.

SCROOGE
Why, it's old Fezziwig! Bless his heart — it's Fezziwig alive again! Dick Wilkins, to be sure!

The dance ends; Fezziwig and his wife shake every hand at the door, wishing all a Merry Christmas.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter, to make these silly folks so full of gratitude. He has spent but a few pounds of your mortal money.

SCROOGE
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome. The happiness he gives is quite as great as if it cost a fortune.

He catches the Spirit's steady gaze and falls silent.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Scrooge, older now — a man in the prime of life, his face already hardening — sits beside BELLE, a fair young woman in mourning dress, tears in her eyes.

BELLE
Another idol has displaced me. A golden one.

YOUNG MAN SCROOGE
This is the even-handed dealing of the world. There is nothing on which it is so hard as poverty, and nothing it professes to condemn with such severity as the pursuit of wealth.

BELLE
You fear the world too much. I release you — with a full heart, for the love of him you once were.

She turns and leaves him standing alone.

INT. BELLE'S PARLOUR - NIGHT

Years later. Belle, now a comely matron, sits by a Christmas fire, her husband and a house full of children around her, boisterous and warm. The HUSBAND turns to her, smiling.

BELLE'S HUSBAND
Belle, I saw an old friend of yours this afternoon. Mr. Scrooge. His partner lies upon the point of death, I hear, and there he sat alone. Quite alone in the world, I do believe.

Scrooge, watching, cannot bear it.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Remove me from this place! I cannot bear it! Leave me! Take me back! Haunt me no longer!

He seizes the Spirit's extinguisher-cap and forces it down over the shrinking light — but the light streams out from under it regardless. He staggers, exhausted, back into his own bed.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Scrooge wakes mid-snore as the bell strikes One again. He throws back his own curtains this time, bracing himself — and light blazes from the next room. He follows it.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS (TRANSFORMED) - NIGHT

The room is a grove of holly, ivy, and mistletoe, heaped with turkeys, geese, puddings, fruit, and steaming punch. On a throne of plenty sits the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT — a vast, jovial giant in a simple green robe, a holly wreath on his brow.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in, and know me better, man! I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me!

SCROOGE
Spirit, conduct me where you will. I learnt a lesson last night which is working now.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe!

EXT. CITY STREETS - DAY

Everything vanishes. Snow, bustling crowds, poulterers' and grocers' windows bright with Christmas goods. The Spirit and Scrooge stand invisible in a baker's doorway, sprinkling incense from the torch onto passers-by's carried dinners.

SCROOGE
Is there a peculiar flavour in what you sprinkle from your torch?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
There is. My own. To any kindly given. To a poor one most — because it needs it most.

SCROOGE
Spirit, I wonder you should desire to cramp these people's opportunities of innocent enjoyment.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
There are some upon this earth of yours who lay claim to know us, and do their deeds of passion, pride, and selfishness in our name, who are as strange to us as if they had never lived. Charge their doings on themselves, not us.

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - DAY

A small, poor, four-roomed house. MRS. CRATCHIT lays the table; BELINDA and PETER CRATCHIT help. Two small Cratchits tumble in, shrieking about the smell of goose. MARTHA CRATCHIT arrives, breathless from her milliner's work. BOB CRATCHIT enters with TINY TIM on his shoulder, the boy's small crutch and iron leg-frame visible.

BOB CRATCHIT
And how did little Tim behave?

MRS. CRATCHIT
As good as gold, and better.

BOB CRATCHIT
He told me, coming home, that he hoped the people saw him in the church, because he was a cripple, and it might be pleasant to them to remember, upon Christmas Day, who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

The goose arrives to great ceremony; then the pudding, blazing in brandy. The family gathers close around the fire.

BOB CRATCHIT (CONT'D)
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us, every one!

Bob holds Tim's small hand, close beside him.

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no! Oh, no, kind Spirit — say he will be spared!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

Scrooge hangs his head, hearing his own words flung back at him.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT (CONT'D)
Forbear that wicked cant, until you have discovered what the surplus is, and where it is. Will you decide what men shall live, what men shall die?

Bob raises his glass.

BOB CRATCHIT
Mr. Scrooge! I'll give you Mr. Scrooge, the Founder of the Feast!

MRS. CRATCHIT
The Founder of the Feast indeed! I wish I had him here — I'd give him a piece of my mind to feast upon.

BOB CRATCHIT
My dear — the children — Christmas Day.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I'll drink his health for your sake and the Day's — not for his.

The children drink the toast half-heartedly; then, the shadow passed, the family grows ten times merrier than before.

INT. FRED'S HOUSE - NIGHT

Warm, bright, full of laughter. FRED holds court by the fire with his wife and their friends, TOPPER and LOUISA among them.

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

LOUISA
More shame for him, Fred!

FRED
He's a comical old fellow — his offences carry their own punishment, and I have nothing to say against him. I mean to give him the same chance every year, whether he likes it or not, for I pity him.

They play games — blind-man's-buff, Yes and No. The whole party bursts into laughter guessing the answer.

LOUISA
I know what it is, Fred! It's your Uncle Scro-o-o-oge!

FRED
He has given us plenty of merriment, I am sure, and it would be ungrateful not to drink his health. Uncle Scrooge!

ALL
Uncle Scrooge!

Scrooge, unseen, unheard, still laughs and claps along, lighter of heart than he has been in years.

EXT. DESOLATE PLACE - NIGHT

A bleak open ground. The Ghost of Christmas Present, visibly grown older, his hair gone grey, opens his robe. Two wretched children cling beneath it — a boy and a girl, gaunt and wolfish.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Oh, Man! Look here. Look, look, down here! This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom, unless the writing be erased.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The bell strikes twelve. The Spirit is gone. A solemn, hooded Phantom of black approaches through the mist.

EXT. LONDON STREET NEAR THE EXCHANGE - DAY

The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME — silent, shrouded, only a pointing hand visible — leads Scrooge among merchants. Two business men, CHUB and SNEDLEY, talk idly.

CHUB
No, I don't know much about it either way. I only know he's dead.

SNEDLEY
What has he done with his money?

CHUB
I haven't heard. Left it to his company, perhaps. He hasn't left it to me. It's likely to be a very cheap funeral, for upon my life I don't know of anybody to go to it.

They laugh and drift apart. Nearby, MR. FARROW and MR. GRIME pass one another.

MR. FARROW
How are you?

MR. GRIME
Old Scratch has got his own at last, hey?

MR. FARROW
So I am told. Cold, isn't it?

Nothing more. They part. Scrooge, baffled, looks to the silent Phantom for meaning and receives only its pointing hand.

INT. OLD JOE'S SHOP - NIGHT

A filthy den of rags, bones, and rusted iron. OLD JOE, ancient and grey, smokes by a charcoal stove. A CHARWOMAN enters with a bundle, followed by MRS. DILBER the laundress, and an undertaker's man close behind.

CHARWOMAN
Let the charwoman alone to be the first! Look here, old Joe, here's a chance!

OLD JOE
Come into the parlour.

They spread their plunder — sleeve-buttons, a brooch, sheets, spoons — and Old Joe chalks sums on the wall.

CHARWOMAN
Open that bundle, Joe.

He unrolls heavy dark cloth.

OLD JOE
What do you call this? Bed-curtains!

CHARWOMAN
Ah! Bed-curtains!

OLD JOE
You don't mean to say you took 'em down, rings and all, with him lying there?

CHARWOMAN
Yes I do. Why not? He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead! Ha, ha, ha!

Scrooge watches in horror.

SCROOGE
Spirit! I see, I see. The case of this unhappy man might be my own. Merciful Heaven, what is this!

INT. DARK BEDROOM - NIGHT

A bare, uncurtained bed. Beneath a ragged sheet lies a still shape. Pale light falls across it. The Phantom's hand points steadily to the head of the bed.

Scrooge trembles at the sheet's edge, unable to lift it.

SCROOGE
Spirit, this is a fearful place. Let us go! If there is any person in this town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me, I beseech you.

INT. HARDWICK PARLOUR - DAY

A modest room. CAROLINE paces anxiously. Her husband, MR. HARDWICK, enters, careworn but strangely relieved.

CAROLINE
Is it good, or bad?

MR. HARDWICK
Bad. He is dead.

CAROLINE
If he relents, there is hope yet!

MR. HARDWICK
He is past relenting. We may sleep to-night with light hearts, Caroline.

She is ashamed of her own relief — and thankful for it all the same.

SCROOGE (V.O.)
Let me see some tenderness connected with a death, or that dark chamber will be for ever present to me.

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - DAY

Quiet. Too quiet. Mrs. Cratchit and the girls sew in silence. Peter reads from a book, then stops. Mrs. Cratchit puts her hand to her face.

MRS. CRATCHIT
The colour hurts my eyes.

She recovers herself.

MRS. CRATCHIT (CONT'D)
It must be near his time. Past it rather.

PETER CRATCHIT
I think he has walked a little slower than he used, these few last evenings, mother.

Bob Cratchit enters, his comforter wound tight. The two young Cratchits climb onto his knees, pressing their cheeks to his face.

BOB CRATCHIT
I have known him walk with Tiny Tim upon his shoulder, very fast indeed. My little, little child!

He breaks down. He goes upstairs alone to a small chair set close beside where the child used to sit, kisses the air where a small face once was, and composes himself before returning.

BOB CRATCHIT (CONT'D)
However and whenever we part from one another, I am sure we shall none of us forget poor Tiny Tim — shall we?

ALL
Never, father.

EXT. CHURCHYARD - DAY

Overgrown graves crowd between close houses. The Phantom stops among them, pointing down at one stone. Scrooge approaches, trembling.

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of things that Will be, or of things that May be, only?

The Phantom does not move. Scrooge creeps forward and reads the name upon the neglected stone: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

SCROOGE (CONT'D)
Am I that man who lay upon the bed? No, Spirit! Oh no, no!

He clutches at the Phantom's robe.

SCROOGE (CONT'D)
I am not the man I was. Why show me this, if I am past all hope! Good Spirit, assure me that I yet may change these shadows you have shown me, by an altered life! I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

He seizes the spectral hand. It shrinks, collapses, dwindling down —

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - DAY

— into a bedpost. Scrooge sits bolt upright in his own bed, in his own room, sunlight pouring through the window.

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me! Oh, Jacob Marley! Heaven, and the Christmas Time be praised for this!

He scrambles out of bed, laughing and crying at once, fumbling with his clothes.

SCROOGE (CONT'D)
I don't know what to do! I am as light as a feather, I am as happy as an angel, I am as merry as a schoolboy! A merry Christmas to everybody! Hallo! Whoop! Hallo!

He flings open the window. Church bells ring out gloriously below. A boy in Sunday clothes loiters in the court.

SCROOGE (CONT'D)
What's to-day, my fine fellow?

BOY
Why, CHRISTMAS DAY.

SCROOGE
I haven't missed it! The Spirits have done it all in one night!

(calling down)

Do you know the Poulterer's in the next street — the one with the prize Turkey, the big one?

BOY
What, the one as big as me?

SCROOGE
Go and buy it! Tell them to bring it here. Come back with the man in five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

The boy sprints off like a shot.

SCROOGE (CONT'D)
I'll send it to Bob Cratchit's! He sha'n't know who sends it. It's twice the size of Tiny Tim!

EXT. STREET - DAY

Scrooge, dressed in his best, walks the bright, cold streets, beaming at everyone he passes. He comes face to face with MR. ASHBY, the charity gentleman from the day before.

SCROOGE
My dear sir. How do you do? I hope you succeeded yesterday. A merry Christmas to you!

MR. ASHBY
Mr. Scrooge?

SCROOGE
Yes. And will you have the goodness —

He whispers a sum into Ashby's ear.

MR. ASHBY
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
Not a farthing less. Will you come and see me?

MR. ASHBY
I will!

INT. FRED'S HOUSE - DAY

Scrooge stands at the dining-room door, hand trembling on the latch, then pushes it open.

SCROOGE
Fred!

FRED
Why bless my soul — who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Fred seizes his hand, pulling him in among the astonished, delighted party — Topper, Louisa, all of them.

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY'S COUNTING-HOUSE - DAY

The next morning. Scrooge sits at his high desk, watching the door, waiting. The church clock strikes nine — then a quarter past. Bob Cratchit hurries in, eighteen minutes late, hat and comforter already half off.

SCROOGE
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time.

SCROOGE
You are. Step this way, sir, if you please.

BOB CRATCHIT
It's only once a year, sir. It shall not be repeated.

SCROOGE
Now, I'll tell you what, my friend — I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer.

He leaps from his stool and jabs Bob in the waistcoat, sending him staggering back.

SCROOGE (CONT'D)
And therefore I am about to raise your salary!

Bob stares, stunned, edging toward the ruler as if for defense.

SCROOGE (CONT'D)
A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas than I have given you for many a year. I'll raise your salary, and assist your struggling family — and we'll discuss it this very afternoon, over a bowl of smoking bishop. Make up the fires, Bob Cratchit, before you dot another i!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Scrooge was better than his word. He did it all, and infinitely more; and to Tiny Tim, who did NOT die, he was a second father.

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - DAY

Tiny Tim, healthy and glowing, sits by the fire among his family, crutch set aside within easy reach.

TINY TIM
God bless us, every one!

FADE OUT.

THE END
